<interrogationes unitas="mensura_quaestio">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>which test sections use the most memory?
    <responsum symbolum="cursus_quaerere_graves CursusGravis" imperium="bin/mensor quaestio">bin/mensor
      quaestio graves [n=N] [titulus=MEMBRUM] [-machina]. Every section
      that fabrica ran with a current credo leaves three facts on its
      own run, in octets: credo.arena_apex (peak use of the section's
      piscina), credo.apex_auctus (how much the process's lifetime peak
      rose while it ran) and credo.vestigium (footprint at its end).
      cursus_quaerere_graves takes the maximum of each across runs per
      section title and orders by the larger of the first two, the
      number a section's memory ceiling judges. titulus= without '#'
      means the sections of that member; n defaults to XX in mensor.
      Sections run outside fabrica, or by a credo older than the
      fourteen-field SECTIO line, have no facts and do not
      appear.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how long did the last merge take, and where did the time go?
    <responsum symbolum="cursus_quaerere_fusio CursusFusio" imperium="bin/mensor quaestio">bin/mensor
      quaestio fusio [arbor=PATH] [-machina]. Since budget B0 (2026-10-27)
      silva.fusio_probare is a run of its own, title 'fusio', so its
      phases and gates are its children. cursus_quaerere_fusio picks the
      latest 'fusio' run of the tree (default: the current one) and the
      latest 'commissio' of the same tree that STARTED before it - the
      merge commit - and lists both runs' children longest first. The
      human view prints the commit block, the fusio block (a run with no
      end shows time so far and 'currit' when its keeper process is
      alive) and a timeline: commit, gap, fusio, total. Merges from
      before B0 have no 'fusio' run and are not found.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>did my performance change actually make test X faster?
    <responsum symbolum="cursus_quaerere_cohortes CursusCohortes CursusAntecessio" imperium="bin/mensor quaestio">Mark
      the commit first: bin/mensor interventio notare -commissum SHA
      -titulus "what changed" [-budget B3] [-scopus a,b]. That records an
      'interventio' run whose body is the titles the commit affects
      (fabrica legentes + gates owed), snapshotted at marking time. Then
      bin/mensor quaestio cohortes titulus=X [post=MS] [arbor=PATH]
      [-machina], run inside the repository. Passing runs of X are split
      by git ANCESTRY, not time: each run lands in the cohort of the
      interventions (affecting X) that its code contains. A run inside a
      commit attempt counts the commit it produced; a dirty run outside
      a commit is omitted (unknown code). Each cohort shows n, median,
      p90 and delta against the largest earlier cohort it extends. The
      'moderatio' line is the control: the median change across the
      latest intervention for leaf titles NO intervention touches - if
      it moved too, the machine got slower or faster, not your code.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
